C = [
("h1", "5. The Lord’s Signs in the Gospels"),
("p", "Claim: the Lord’s signs are given to Israel in the land, and the Gospels record what they were for, who saw them, and how Israel answered. John calls the Lord’s miracles signs and says why he wrote them down. The Lord refuses to give a sign on demand to the scribes and Pharisees and names one sign, Jonah’s. Both lines of evidence stand in the text, and the volume holds them together without turning either into a general statement about signs."),
("sub", "5.1 What John records"),
("p", "John closes the first account with a statement of purpose: “This beginning of miracles did Jesus in Cana of Galilee, and manifested forth his glory; and his disciples believed on him” (John 2:11). The KJV renders the word as “miracles”; the word is the one the Greek text of John uses for a sign. The Lord’s first sign is done in Galilee, it manifests His glory, and it ends with the belief of His disciples."),
("p", "Others read the signs and drew a conclusion. Nicodemus comes by night and says, “Rabbi, we know that thou art a teacher come from God: for no man can do these miracles that thou doest, except God be with him” (John 3:2). The people who resorted to Him beyond Jordan said, “John did no miracle: but all things that John spake of this man were true” (John 10:41). In both statements the miracles are the ground of a conclusion about the Lord’s person, and the speakers are Israelites."),
("p", "The Gospel also records the demand for signs. The Lord says to the nobleman of Capernaum, “Except ye see signs and wonders, ye will not believe” (John 4:48). The crowd that had been fed asks Him, “What sign shewest thou then, that we may see, and believe thee? what dost thou work?” (John 6:30). They ask for a sign immediately after one. And the chief priests and Pharisees in council say, “What do we? for this man doeth many miracles” (John 11:47). The council admits the miracles and plans His death. John’s comment at the close of chapter 12 stands as the verdict of the narrative: “But though he had done so many miracles before them, yet they believed not on him” (John 12:37)."),
("sub", "5.2 John’s stated purpose"),
("p", "John writes near the end: “And many other signs truly did Jesus in the presence of his disciples, which are not written in this book: But these are written, that ye might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God; and that believing ye might have life through his name” (John 20:30, 31). The statement gives John’s own reason for the signs he selected. The signs were done “in the presence of his disciples,” they are not all written, and the ones that are written are written for belief and for life “through his name.”"),
("p", "The passage states what John wrote and why. It does not say that signs are the means of belief for every later reader in every age, and it does not say that signs will be repeated. The volume reads it as John’s reason for selecting the signs in his book. The verse is cited in chapter 11, where an objector uses it, and answered there."),
("sub", "5.3 The sign of Jonah"),
("p", "The scribes and Pharisees ask: “Master, we would see a sign from thee” (Matthew 12:38). The Lord answers: “An evil and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given to it, but the sign of the prophet Jonas: For as Jonas was three days and three nights in the whale’s belly; so shall the Son of man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth” (Matthew 12:39, 40). The answer is not a refusal of every sign, because He names one. He refuses the request as it was made, for a sign “from thee” on demand, and promises the sign that will be given. The sign is His three days and three nights in the heart of the earth."),
("p", "The demand is repeated in a later chapter in a slightly different form. “The Pharisees also with the Sadducees came, and tempting desired him that he would shew them a sign from heaven” (Matthew 16:1). The Lord says, “O ye hypocrites, ye can discern the face of the sky; but can ye not discern the signs of the times? A wicked and adulterous generation seeketh after a sign; and there shall no sign be given unto it, but the sign of the prophet Jonas. And he left them, and departed” (Matthew 16:3, 4). In Luke the Lord says, “This is an evil generation: they seek a sign; and there shall no sign be given it, but the sign of Jonas the prophet. For as Jonas was a sign unto the Ninevites, so shall also the Son of man be to this generation” (Luke 11:29, 30)."),
("p", "Three observations follow from the three accounts. The Lord has already given miracles, and the demand comes after them; the demand is for another kind of proof, “from heaven.” The accounts name a generation, “this generation,” as the one that seeks. And the sign named in reply is a person: Jonah is “a sign unto the Ninevites,” and the Son of man will be a sign “to this generation.” The sign is addressed to Israel’s leaders and to their generation."),
("p", "Herod’s case stands apart: “And when Herod saw Jesus, he was exceeding glad: for he was desirous to see him of a long season, because he had heard many things of him; and he hoped to have seen some miracle done by him” (Luke 23:8). The Lord answers him nothing. The sign is withheld from the man who wants to see it as a spectacle. The text states the wish and the silence."),
("sub", "5.4 The works that answered John"),
("p", "John the Baptist, in prison, sends two of his disciples with a question: “Art thou he that should come, or do we look for another?” (Matthew 11:3). The Lord’s answer is a list of works: “Go and shew John again those things which ye do hear and see: The blind receive their sight, and the lame walk, the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, the dead are raised up, and the poor have the gospel preached to them. And blessed is he, whosoever shall not be offended in me” (Matthew 11:4–6). The list draws on Isaiah. “Then the eyes of the blind shall be opened, and the ears of the deaf shall be unstopped. Then shall the lame man leap as an hart, and the tongue of the dumb sing” (Isaiah 35:5, 6). The Lord answers the question of who He is with the works the prophets named for the coming one."),
("p", "At Nazareth He reads the place in Isaiah that speaks of “recovering of sight to the blind” and closes with the statement, “This day is this scripture fulfilled in your ears” (Luke 4:18, 21). In Jerusalem He tells the Jews who ask Him to say plainly whether He is the Christ, “I told you, and ye believed not: the works that I do in my Father’s name, they bear witness of me” (John 10:25). He adds, “If I do not the works of my Father, believe me not. But if I do, though ye believe not me, believe the works: that ye may know, and believe, that the Father is in me, and I in him” (John 10:37, 38). The works are given as witness, the witness is given to Israel, and the question they answer is the question of who He is."),
("sub", "5.5 Peter’s summary"),
("p", "At Pentecost Peter summarizes the Lord’s ministry for the men who had seen it: “Ye men of Israel, hear these words; Jesus of Nazareth, a man approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs, which God did by him in the midst of you, as ye yourselves also know” (Acts 2:22). The sentence names the hearers, “ye men of Israel.” It names the agent, “God … by him.” It names the place, “in the midst of you.” And it appeals to what the hearers knew: “as ye yourselves also know.” The Lord’s signs were done before Israel and are claimed as evidence in front of Israel."),
("ctbl", "The Lord’s signs and what the Gospels say of them.", [
    ("Passage", "Who speaks or receives", "What the text states"),
    ("John 2:11", "John", "The beginning of the miracles; His glory; the disciples believed"),
    ("John 3:2; 10:41", "Nicodemus; the people beyond Jordan", "The miracles are read as the mark of one from God"),
    ("John 4:48; 6:30", "The Lord; the crowd", "The demand for signs before belief"),
    ("John 11:47; 12:37", "The council; John", "Many miracles; the council plans; the people did not believe"),
    ("John 20:30, 31", "John", "The signs written, and the purpose for which they are written"),
    ("Matthew 12:38–40; 16:1–4; Luke 11:29, 30", "The Lord to the scribes and Pharisees", "No sign but the sign of Jonah"),
    ("Luke 23:8", "Herod", "Desire for a miracle; the Lord answers nothing"),
    ("Matthew 11:2–6; Luke 4:18–21; John 10:25, 37, 38", "The Lord to John’s disciples, the synagogue and the Jews", "The works are the witness Isaiah named, and they answer who He is"),
    ("Acts 2:22", "Peter to the men of Israel", "Approved of God by miracles, wonders and signs"),
  ], [0.28, 0.27, 0.45]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The Lord’s signs are done in the land before Israel and are cited as proof to Israel.",
    "The Gospels record two responses: conclusions drawn about His person, and demands for more.",
    "John states the purpose of the signs he selected and does not extend the statement to every sign or every age.",
    "The Lord answers John’s question with the works Isaiah named and cites the works as witness to Israel.",
    "The Lord withholds a sign on demand from an evil generation and names the sign of Jonah.",
    "The sign of Jonah is addressed to that generation, and the Lord himself ties it to the Son of man’s three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.",
  ]),
]
